        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "feat: clarify iOS Location Always permission flow",
        "body": "Related: #99229\nRelated: #86217\nRelated closed candidate: #99235\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nResolves a problem where iOS users could select Location Always in OpenClaw without the app clearly explaining whether iOS had actually granted background-capable location access. That made wake-up/location workflows ambiguous, especially when iOS only allowed While Using, denied access, or global Location Services were disabled.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThis draft adds an explicit iOS permission summary for Location settings that separates the user's selected OpenClaw mode from the effective iOS authorization state. It also wires the background-location contract into the native configuration and location service path: background updates are only enabled for Always-capable use, and significant-change monitoring is stopped when the mode is disabled or downgraded.\n\nThis is intentionally a draft for product/privacy/design review. It follows the current SettingsProTab pattern for now because #99231 may reshape the Settings UI.\n\n#99235 explored a similar area but was closed after it expanded beyond the Location Always flow. This PR keeps the scope narrower: Location permission copy, native i18n inventory, Info.plist background location declaration, background/significant-change monitoring gates, and focused tests.\n\n## User Impact\n\nUsers get clearer feedback when Location Always is selected but iOS has not granted background-capable permission. Operators and reviewers also get a cleaner contract for when the iOS app may use background location behavior.\n\nUse cases covered in this draft:\n\n- Keep OpenClaw reachable for location-triggered/background wake-up workflows when the user explicitly selects Always and iOS grants Always.\n- Show a warning when Always is selected but iOS only grants While Using, so users understand background location will not work.\n- Show a warning when location is denied, restricted, or globally disabled in iOS settings.\n- Stop background/significant-change monitoring when the user turns Location off or downgrades away from Always.\n- Reconcile iOS Settings downgrades through CoreLocation delegate updates, including persisted significant-change monitor cleanup after process relaunch.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Rebased on current `upstream/main` before the latest push.\n- `pnpm native:i18n:check` passed (`entries=2398 changed=false`).\n- Targeted SwiftFormat lint passed for the changed Location/Services/test files.\n- Targeted SwiftLint passed for the changed Location/Services/test files.\n- `git diff --check` passed.\n- Focused simulator tests passed: `OpenClawTests/LocationPermissionSummaryTests` on iPhone 17 / iOS 26.5, 9 tests.\n- Built simulator app Info.plist includes `UIBackgroundModes = [audio, location, remote-notification]`.\n- Local Codex review finding on persisted significant-change monitoring was accepted/fixed: downgrade cleanup now always unregisters the OS monitor instead of trusting only in-process state.\n- Local Codex review after that fix found no discrete correctness issues in the changed location permission/background monitoring flow.\n\nNo physical-device proof yet; this is a draft review PR for privacy/product/design review before we decide whether to finish it.\n\nAI-assisted: yes, Codex.\n",
